David Thom wrote:
> 
> Where can I find info on which (non-Compaq) SCSI disks are supported by
> AlphaServer 1000 running 4.0E?
> 
> And where's the docs on how to configure them? (for that matter, where is
> the documentation on installing Compaq's disks?!)

Should be able to use any SCSI disk.  Just check to see what sort of
SCSI cables you have (presumably wide) and either order a drive that
matches or be sure you have the right adapter.

When you plug in the drive, the first thing you might want to do is go
to the firmware prompt (">>>") and type "show dev", which will list the
devices the computer sees.  That will tell you which SCSI bus the drive
is on (if you have more than one bus) and what the drive ID number on
that bus is.  It might be listed as dkb600, in which case it's ID#6 on
the second bus (bus "b").  If the drive doesn't show up there you
probably have a cabling or SCSI problem like incorrect termination. 
Some Alphas come with cables that have terminators built into them,
which nicely eliminates the headaches of worrying about termination
jumpers when you rearrange disks.

After you boot up into Digital Unix, run the "diskconfig" program, which
lets you set up disk partitions.  Diskconfig should list all the drives
attached to your system.  After you configure the partitions, what you
do depends on what sort of filesystem you are setting up.  For UFS, use
the "newfs" command to create a filesystem on a particular partition. 
For advfs, if you're not already familiar with how advfs works, it's
probably best to read the general description of advfs by looking at the
man page ("man advfs").

Also, a handy utility when troubleshooting SCSI disks is scu (see the
