[Posted and mailed]

In article <D1A6297322647B2C.984D1C652F826FB6.D592C669B4C29BA2@lp.airnews.net>,
	Brad Felmey <bfe369@airmail.net> writes:
> CDE has got to go. I've got an old AS200 4/233, and I sprung the $99 a while
> back for the Tru64 v5 discs from Compaq. I've finally got some time to work on
> learning it, and I just can't *stand* CDE. It's simply awful.

You can use KDE (1.12) on Tru64 Unix without too much problem.

Look at http://www.kde.org and search for binary distributions.  Hopefully,
they'll release a Tru64 port of 2.0 when it is released.

Once you have KDE, you can start it up by putting the filename of the
session manager (/usr/local/kde/bin/startkde) into your
$HOME/.xsession file.


GNOME has also been ported to Tru64, but there isn't any "clean"
binary distribution of the environment, except bits and pieces on the
Freeware CD.  This will probably change with Compaq's funding of the
GNOME foundation.


Both KDE and GNOME are attempts for a better desktop environment.  If
you just want a bare-bones window manager, Tru64 ships with
/usr/bin/X11/twm.  Motif (classic) is available at /usr/bin/X11/mwm.

There are many other window managers that will build on Tru64.  I
personally use fvwm2 (ftp://ftp.fvwm.org/).

The secret to all of these is your $HOME/.xsession file; it's a script
of commands that get executed when your display manager opens your
session.

