In article <Pine.SOL.3.96.1000822113702.404U-100000@thale>,
	Keith Bradnam <keith@thale.nott.ac.uk> writes:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> I have recently installed the apache web server on our XP1000 (running
> Tru64 5.0a).
> 
> If I want the httpd process to fire up whenever the machine is booted, do
> I have to:
> 
> a) Just add an entry into /etc/inittab

  No, you need not.
 
> and/or
> 
> b) Add an entry somewhere in /sbin/rc3.d ?
> If so, could it just be something as simple as:
> 
> #!/sbin/sh
> /usr/local/apache_1.3.12/src/httpd

  Best way would be to create a script, that starts the daemon and
  put it in /sbin/init.d. In /sbin/rc3.d there usually aren't any
  scripts, but only links like, e.g. S00inet@ -> ../init.d/inet.

  If you switch to runlevel X, then all the scripts in rcX.d are
  executed. First the ones that start with a K (for kill) and then
  the ones that start with an S (for start).

  The scripts are called with "stop" as an argument if the link begins
  with K or "start" if it begins with S. The scripts execute in the
  order you get them with an ls, so the number is important. Your httpd
  script should come somewhere after basic network and stuff has been
  started.

  If you want to be real tidy, then you can put a K-link into
  /sbin/rc2.d, so your script can stop the httpd gets, when you change
  to runlevel 2 (multiuser state without network services).

