Keith Bradnam wrote:

> Hi,
>
> I have recently installed the apache web server on our XP1000 (running
> Tru64 5.0a).
>
> If I want the httpd process to fire up whenever the machine is booted, do
> I have to:
>
> a) Just add an entry into /etc/inittab
>
> and/or
>
> b) Add an entry somewhere in /sbin/rc3.d ?
> If so, could it just be something as simple as:
>
> #!/sbin/sh
> /usr/local/apache_1.3.12/src/httpd
>
> ???
>
> Keith
>
> P.S. There was a very old newsgroup posting relating to this, but it's
> from 1997 so I thought I'd check to see if anything has changed since
> then.
>
> ~  Keith Bradnam - Developer, Arabidopsis Genome Resource (AGR)
> ~  Nottingham Arabidopsis Stock Centre - http://nasc.nott.ac.uk/
> ~  University Park, University of Nottingham, NG7 2RD, UK
> ~  Tel: (0115) 951 3091

answer is b) +

The script in /sbin (rc0, rc2 and rc3) are call at init time (0: halt, 2:
shutdown (single user) or 3: boot multi-user)
executing all the script in directories /sbin/rc0.d, /sbin/rc2.d or
/sbin/rc3.d in order starting by K (for kill, I suppose),
and S after (for start, I suppose again)

This files are links to scripts files (in /sbin/init.d) and are call with
arguments 'stop' if  starting by K
and 'start' if starting by S.

See /sbin/init.d/syslog (for example).

So you must create two links and one script (whith two args: start and
stop).











