Keith Bradnam <keith@thale.nott.ac.uk> writes:
> Our apache web server (running on Tru64 5.0a) starts on server boot-up.
[...]
> One of our scripts (which sometimes needs a lot of memory, i.e. higher
> than the default 'datasize' limit) occasionally fails.  
> 
> Is there a way of changing the default limits for a user such as 
> 'nobody'??? 

Two other possibilities that might be easier:

1) change the limit in the script that is failing--if it's a /bin/sh
script, use the 'ulimit -d' command; for a csh script, 'limit data'.
If it's not a shell script, there may be a way to change the limit in
the language that it is in, or you could wrap it in a shell script
that does something like

#! /bin/sh
ulimit -d 4242424
exec real-script "$@"

2) add a 'limit -d' command to the init.d script that starts apache.
All the children will inherit the higher limit.

