LONG ANSWER:

The system doesn't think there are two tape drives.
The characteristic you are describing was created in part to support clustering.
In the old naming scheme, /dev/rz0c or whatever, the device name was dependent on
the physical location of the drive, and if you moved the drive to a new location,
the system would not recognize that it is in fact the same drive.

In the new naming scheme, /devices/disk/dskxx whatever, the system keeps a list
of all disks it has ever seen, numbering them sequentially, regardless of the
physical location.  This way, if you disconnect a drive from the local SCSI bus,
and connect it to a storage array 10km away that's linked by fibre, it will
appear to the system as the same device in the same location.

While this is not particularly helpful on a DS20, take a GS-system for example.
If you've got 32 processors, connected to a storage array through fibre channel
in switched fabric mode, you've got a virtually unlimited number of functionally
equivalent paths between the device and the various CPUs.  If the device names
were dependent on the data path, .... well...  That poor computer would be doing
things poor computers were never meant to do.

SHORT ANSWER:
The system doesn't think there are two tape drives.  It automatically assigns the
same device name to the same device, no matter where you connect it.  It is
nearly impossible to change the device name manually.  If you ever replace this
drive with a new one, you will have the same problem all over again.  I suggest
you modify your script(s) and things to use any device name of your choice.


