use a lot of memory for the UBC (Unified Buffer Cache) to cache both
data and text.  This makes re-use of these items much faster because it
does not need to be fetched from disk.  However, large chunks of the UBC
are flushed (and this is tunable) the instant another process needs more
memory and can't find any.  The question you have to ask is if you want
to include UBC memory as memory being used by the O/S.  The answer is
"yes, but .....".

Tony V.
Kernel Group



