Licensing in Tru64 is based on PAK's.One PAK (Product Authorization Key) is
a sheet containing this info:

ISSUER:
AUTH. NUMBER:
PROD. NAME:
PRODUCER:
NUM. OF UNITS:
PROD. RELEASE DATE:
KEY TERM. DATE:
AVAILAB. TABLE CODE:
ACTIVITY TABLE CODE:
KEY OPTIONS:
PROD. TOKEN:
HW ID:
CHECKSUM:

Not all the info are needed (depends on license type), but for OSF-BASE (I
think your case)
yuo must have: ISSUER, AUTH.NUM., PROD.,PRODUCER, UNITS, AVAILAB. CODE, KEY
OPT., CHECKSUM.


> Q #2: I want to get one to recognize the other, via ftp.

#man ftp (ftp is default installed in Tru64)
installing TCP/IP prot. in W2000 you can connect them together.

Other way, mount unix disks in W2000 via NFS, but you need an NFS client on
W2000 or a Linux machine bridge, that can mount the two file systems type.

> Q #3:
To connect as root via telnet in a unix machine, you must allow the PTY
ports, adding at the end of file
/etc/securettys simply the word "ptys" and saving it. (don't use CDE editor,
use vi or it won't work)

If you telnet to same machine you are working, TCP use the loopback address,
so almost "nothing" comes out from NIC.

