> If their jobs use more than 1.7 GB the machine is getting very slow.

Makes sense.  The O.S. needs some room to breathe, after all.  But you
can minimize this requirement by adjusting your filesystem-buffer-cache
parameters to the bare minimum (see the tuning guide, and remember that
the UBC params s/b adjusted en masse -- don't just snipe at 1 or 2 of
the UBC params because that can get you into big trouble).  You may wish
to avail yourself of the sys_check tool, which s/b run while the system
is misbehaving, although it sounds as though your system is tuned so
poorly that this would be like trying to use a micrometer to measure the
length of a football field.

> Compared with an SGI-WS with the same Software they don't have this
> problem.

You've identified brand differences but that's all.  That's not enough
information WRT to the 2 machines' guts (memory, I/O subsystem, etc.),
configuration, operating systems and patches, applications, and so on
even to *begin* to supply the information needed to do a scientific
comparison.

> Is the dec acting differently when it swapps?

Wrong question.  Your question should be, "Why is the DEC swapping and
what can I do about it?"  You'll be well on the path to obtaining the
answer if you follow my advice above.


