Johnny's advice about using the sys_check tool to flag system bottle
necks is excellent.

As far Even's question about swapping, there are 2 selectable modes of
swapping called "lazy allocation mode" and "eager allocation mode".  

In eager mode, swap space is RESERVED for a process before it starts and
if not enough space is available, the process is not started.

In lazy mode, a process will start regardless of the amount of swap
space inuse/available.  Space is allocated when it is needed.  If not
enough swap space is available, the process will be terminated.

Tony V.
Kernel Group



