In article <8me4hu$8i3$1@pheidippides.axion.bt.co.uk>, Hin-Tak@pc7221.btlabs.bt.co.uk (hin-tak.leung@bt.com) writes:

>well, I am sure you can do:
>
>	find . -type f -exec rm {} \;
>

This is likely to take much longer than:

	find . -type f -print | xargs rm

because, the first one will have to start a new rm for each
file.  The 2nd one will use one rm for a whole bunch of 
files.  While UNIX forks are relatively cheap, they do take
time, I/O and page faults.

In the end, it will still be I/O bound though.
>

--
